  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nAny fog Thursday morning will dissipate within a few hours of \nsunrise and as a drier post-frontal airmass slowly infiltrates the \nregion as a surface high pressure center enters the Mid-Mississippi \nRiver Valley. Although the airmass will also be seasonably cool, \ngreater insolation will lead to much of the CWA seeing high \ntemperatures warmer than today and in the 80s F. The high pressure \ncenter will dominate the region into Friday morning, providing \nfavorable conditions for at least additional patchy fog in river \nvalleys late Thursday night/Friday morning.\n\nIn prevailing upper-level northwesterly flow, a shortwave trough \nwill force a weak cold front through the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley sometime in the Friday evening-Saturday morning timeframe. \nGiven limited moisture return, weak frontal converge, and frontal \npassage out of phase with peak daytime heat; only scattered showers \nand a thunderstorm or two is forecast. Accordingly, ensemble model \nguidance has 20 to 30 percent probabilities of measurable rainfall \nwith the front. The main impact of the front will instead be another \nseasonably cool post-frontal airmass arriving in its wake, leading \nto continued near to slightly below average temperatures at \nleast through the weekend.\n\nSunday through early to mid-next week, upper-level northwesterly \nflow will continue, but global model guidance have slight \ndifferences in the position of longwave troughing over the Great \nLakes and ridging across the Rocky Mountains into Southern Plains \nand which feature has greater influence on the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley. Between these features, there will be a wavering baroclinic \nzone separating a hot airmass to the southwest and a seasonably cool \nairmass to the northeast, resulting in the NBM interquartile range \nspanning below average to above average temperatures (5 to 8 F \ndifference). To further complicate the temperature forecast, \nepisodic showers and thunderstorms are favored to develop/track \nalong the baroclinic zone at times from Sunday night into early \nTuesday as multiple shortwave troughs pass.",
